WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:05.609
The Marvel Cinematic Universe adds another
hero to its ranks in "Thor: Love and Thunder,"

00:00:05.609 --> 00:00:10.420
as Jane Foster transforms into the Mighty
Thor while battling stage four cancer.

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:13.160
Are fans ready to discuss Jane's fate?

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:15.140
And how does it compare to the comics?

00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:16.680
Keep watching to find out.

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:18.560
"Let's bring the rainbow!"

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:23.180
The world-renowned astrophysicist hasn't been
seen in the present-day MCU — aside from

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:27.480
a quick cameo in "Avengers: Endgame" — since
"Thor: The Dark World," and she's back with

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:28.630
a thunderous bang.

00:00:28.630 --> 00:00:33.220
Her new Asgardian powers are a shock to Thor
himself, who tries to look at his own place

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:36.149
in the cosmos in something of a mid-life crisis.

00:00:36.149 --> 00:00:40.440
When speaking to Fandango, director Taika
Waititi revealed how he convinced Natalie

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:44.670
Portman to return, noting that he wanted to
reinvent the role for the star.

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:48.910
"Her character in those first few films is
probably not the most exciting version of

00:00:48.910 --> 00:00:51.340
the female character we want from these films.

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:55.510
[...] I wanted to change that character just
like we'd changed Thor's character for 'Ragnarok'

00:00:55.510 --> 00:01:00.280
and give her more license to be adventurous
and fun and funny, 'cause Natalie's a really

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:01.600
funny person."

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:05.830
Foster handles herself well in the fight against
Gorr the God Butcher, who's determined to

00:01:05.830 --> 00:01:10.140
slaughter all the gods in the universe, but
she also has to deal with the fact that — as

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:15.460
in the comics — using her powers is effectively
stopping her chemotherapy from doing its job.

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:19.420
It puts Jane in an incredibly difficult position
because although she wants to be there in

00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:22.430
the fight, she has to slow down because of
her health.

00:01:22.430 --> 00:01:27.200
Ultimately, this leads to an incredibly emotional
finale for Jane and Thor — one that could

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:28.600
lead to something more.

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:32.850
Jane, unfortunately, succumbs to her cancer
in the final battle because the energy she

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:36.060
uses to help Thor fight Gorr drains her strength.

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:40.409
Thankfully, her soul is taken up to Valhalla
in the post-credits scene because she died

00:01:40.409 --> 00:01:43.670
on the field of battle — a sign that she's
a true hero.

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:48.450
Obviously, it's a shame that she dies only
one film into her superhero career, but it

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:50.990
is a worthy evolution for the talented scientist.

00:01:50.990 --> 00:01:52.860
But is it actually the end?

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:56.650
In the Jason Aaron comics from which parts
of Jane's story in "Thor: Love and Thunder"

00:01:56.650 --> 00:02:01.869
were adapted, the focus is on Jane's new identity,
but she does eventually die from her cancer

00:02:01.869 --> 00:02:06.230
after saving Asgardia in "Mighty Thor" #706.

00:02:06.230 --> 00:02:11.489
As she stands at the gates, though, Odin decides
that her sacrifice is a feat of true heroism

00:02:11.489 --> 00:02:14.019
and channels a cosmic thunderstorm to resurrect
her.

00:02:14.019 --> 00:02:16.680
A superhero resurrected from death?

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:17.680
In comics?

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:18.999
Surely not!

00:02:18.999 --> 00:02:23.579
So yes, it's entirely possible that Jane might
return to the MCU from Valhalla in a future

00:02:23.579 --> 00:02:24.579
film.

00:02:24.579 --> 00:02:27.989
Whether she should come back from the dead
is another question entirely, because this

00:02:27.989 --> 00:02:32.090
wraps up her storyline nicely while honoring
what makes Jane great as a person.

00:02:32.090 --> 00:02:36.609
But Marvel always lays the groundwork for
the future, and the Valhalla post-credits

00:02:36.609 --> 00:02:38.309
scene isn't there for nothing.

00:02:38.309 --> 00:02:42.790
Where could Jane's story in the MCU go next,
though, and what are the chances that Portman

00:02:42.790 --> 00:02:44.099
returns to the role?

00:02:44.099 --> 00:02:48.639
You might think that after dealing with stage
four cancer, taking on the powers of a Norse

00:02:48.639 --> 00:02:53.909
god, and dying valiantly in battle, only to
be revived again by Odin himself, Jane Foster

00:02:53.909 --> 00:02:56.879
might be ready to call it quits on her superhero
career.

00:02:56.879 --> 00:03:01.470
However, in the world of Marvel Comics, the
character continues to play a noteworthy role

00:03:01.470 --> 00:03:02.670
in the action.

00:03:02.670 --> 00:03:06.530
She pops up throughout the "War of the Realms"
arc and even assumes the mantle of a Valkyrie

00:03:06.530 --> 00:03:09.870
in the aftermath of the bloody interdimensional
battle.

00:03:09.870 --> 00:03:14.370
Although the MCU has obviously been forging
its own path through the Marvel canon, using

00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:19.360
the comics as inspiration, rather than doctrine,
Jane's robust and continuing story on the

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:23.239
page certainly leaves the door wide open for
her return down the road.

00:03:23.239 --> 00:03:26.900
The character's buddy-buddy friendship with
King Valkyrie in "Thor: Love and Thunder"

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:32.339
could be one such conduit for a return, especially
considering Jane's transformation into one

00:03:32.339 --> 00:03:34.609
of the Norse ferrywomen in her comics arc.

00:03:34.609 --> 00:03:39.329
Of course, she only becomes a Valkyrie after
the others are killed, so if the relationship

00:03:39.329 --> 00:03:44.269
between the two characters does bring Jane
back to the MCU, it could end in tragedy.

00:03:44.269 --> 00:03:46.430
But we're getting ahead of ourselves.

00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:50.489
As far as any future live-action appearances
from Jane are concerned, the real deciding

00:03:50.489 --> 00:03:55.359
factor will most likely be whether Natalie
Portman is interested in doing another MCU

00:03:55.359 --> 00:03:56.359
film.

00:03:56.359 --> 00:04:00.769
After stepping away from the franchise following
2013's "Thor: The Dark World," Taika Waititi

00:04:00.769 --> 00:04:04.999
helped convince Portman to return for "Love
and Thunder" by breathing new life into the

00:04:04.999 --> 00:04:06.669
character of Jane Foster.

00:04:06.669 --> 00:04:11.299
Perhaps with another juicy storyline, Portman
could be convinced to return again.

